# Most of what follows can be set in the console instead: Settings (the gear in
# the sidebar) changes it for the deployment or one workspace, and a value saved
# there wins over the one here, which stays the default. What has to be here:
# model access, the store, sign-in, the computer's name and key, shell
# isolation, web search on your own endpoint, and the two cron schedules. See
# docs/configuration.md.
# BOT_SETTINGS_EDITABLE=off

# ─── Models ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Routed through the Vercel AI Gateway. On Vercel, a linked project can use OIDC
# instead of a key: run `vercel link && vercel env pull`.
AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# Each workspace picks its models on the console's Models page (free ones
# included); these are the defaults until it does. BOT_MODELS_EDITABLE=off makes
# that page read-only, so only these apply.
# BOT_MODELS_EDITABLE=off
# BOT_HQ_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
# BOT_HQ_REASONING=low
# AI Gateway data controls, on every Gateway call: Zero Data Retention (only
# providers that keep nothing; Pro and Enterprise teams), and inference kept in
# one region (Gateway fails a call it cannot serve there). See docs/safety.md.
# BOT_ZDR=1
# BOT_INFERENCE_REGION=us
# Jev, TypeSafe's decision model on AI Gateway, answers the small questions a
# Bot system has to ask — is this page a sign-in wall, does this result meet
# the brief — as a probability rather than prose, for a fraction of a model
# turn. Today every answer is recorded and acted on by nobody: `npm run
# jev:report` shows what it would have decided against what happened. It needs
# a Gateway key, so it is off on its own on your own model endpoint, and every
# use works the same way without it. Off turns it off outright.
# BOT_JEV=off
# BOT_JEV_MODEL=typesafe-ai/jev
# How concentrated an answer must be before a caller may act on it (0-1).
# BOT_JEV_CONFIDENCE=0.6
# How long page_pilot may drive before it hands back to the bot's own model.
# BOT_PILOT_SECONDS=60
# The timezone a routine's clock schedule uses when nobody names one.
# BOT_TIMEZONE=America/New_York
# HQ rates each job quick | standard | deep, and the job runs on that level's model.
# Prefer models that neither retain nor train on prompts: Bots read inboxes.
# BOT_MODEL_QUICK=alibaba/qwen3.8-flash
# BOT_MODEL_STANDARD=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
# BOT_MODEL_DEEP=anthropic/claude-opus-5
# One model for every job, ignoring the levels above.
# BOT_TEAMMATE_MODEL=
# Teammate reasoning depth: medium (default) | low | high | xhigh
# BOT_TEAMMATE_REASONING=medium

# Your own model endpoint instead of AI Gateway: any API that speaks OpenAI's
# chat completions format (Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM, OpenRouter, a company proxy).
# Every model name above is then sent there; BOT_MODEL is used wherever no
# role- or level-specific variable is set. Reasoning is left to the provider
# unless BOT_HQ_REASONING / BOT_TEAMMATE_REASONING is set, and the USD spend caps
# below cannot price these models.
# BOT_MODEL_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# BOT_MODEL=qwen3:8b
# BOT_MODEL_API_KEY=
# BOT_MODEL_CONTEXT_TOKENS=128000
# What those models cost, so the USD spend caps below hold on your endpoint:
# model=input/output in USD per million tokens, comma-separated; `*` prices any
# other model. On OpenRouter the price list is read automatically. A model with
# no price counts as free, and the token caps below stop a runaway loop instead.
# BOT_MODEL_PRICES=anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5=3/15,*=0/0
# BOT_HQ_INPUT_TOKEN_LIMIT=40000000
# BOT_HQ_OUTPUT_TOKEN_LIMIT=2000000
# BOT_JOB_INPUT_TOKEN_LIMIT=10000000
# BOT_JOB_OUTPUT_TOKEN_LIMIT=500000
# Web search on your own endpoint. eve's built-in search runs through AI
# Gateway; with BOT_MODEL_BASE_URL set, Bots search through one of these
# instead, or with their browser when none is configured.
#   searxng: your own SearXNG at BOT_SEARCH_URL (docker-compose.yml runs one)
#   brave | tavily | exa: hosted, with BOT_SEARCH_API_KEY
# BOT_SEARCH_PROVIDER=searxng
# BOT_SEARCH_URL=http://localhost:8888
# BOT_SEARCH_API_KEY=
# The generalist every workspace starts with.
# BOT_DEFAULT_BOT_NAME=Atlas

# ─── Persistence ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The roster, the job queue, the activity feed, and artifacts.
# Driver: blob | fs | memory. Auto-detected when unset (Blob on Vercel, disk locally).
# BOT_STORE=fs
# BOT_DATA_DIR=.data
# BOT_STORE_PREFIX=bot/v1

# Vercel Blob. On Vercel, connect a store and OIDC supplies the credentials:
#   vercel blob store add bot-store
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=
# BLOB_STORE_ID=

# Memory slots (profile, team, craft) use eve's file-memory provider and keep
# their documents in the store above, next to the rest of the app's data.

# ─── Workspaces ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every record is scoped to a workspace resolved from the caller's auth.
BOT_DEFAULT_WORKSPACE=default

# ─── Ops channel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Who can open the console. On Vercel, BOT_CONSOLE_TOKEN is the password you sign
# in with (the Deploy button asks for it). Or put the deployment behind Vercel
# Authentication for All Deployments: the app checks that its address is
# protected and lets the project's owner straight in. Vercel's default Standard
# Protection leaves the production address public, so it does not count. Each
# token is bound to one workspace, so a token never grants another. With
# neither, only a local dev server is open.
# Accept console tokens only, even behind Vercel Authentication:
# BOT_CONSOLE_AUTH=token
# BOT_CONSOLE_TOKEN=
# BOT_CONSOLE_TOKENS=token-a=acme,token-b=globex
# BOT_CONSOLE_OPEN=0

# ─── Dispatcher ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Jobs wait for their own start times inside run_job; this cron is only the
# watchdog that re-dispatches anything left behind. Daily by default, the most
# Vercel Hobby allows; on Pro, "* * * * *" picks up stragglers within a minute.
# BOT_TICK_CRON=17 6 * * *
# Vercel Pro, production: a background worker (daemon/, a Vercel Daemon declared
# in vercel.json) does the watchdog's work every 30 seconds, so nothing waits for
# a console or the cron. About 3 Blob operations a pass (~$1-2/month on Pro).
# Off by default and never on Hobby: it would use up Blob's monthly operations in
# hours, and Vercel then blocks the store for 30 days. Redeploy after changing it.
# BOT_WORKER=on
# BOT_WORKER_EVERY_SECONDS=30
# BOT_TICK_BATCH=10
# BOT_ACTIVITY_RETAIN=500
# The daily standup. Crons are UTC (Vercel evaluates them in UTC) and fixed at
# build time; 13:00 UTC on weekdays is 9am in New York in summer, 8am in winter.
# It posts to HQ's desk; BOT_STANDUP_ROOM may name a Bot's room (bot-<id>)
# instead. Any other room is not shown in the console, so it falls back to desk.
# BOT_STANDUP_CRON=0 13 * * 1-5
# BOT_STANDUP_ROOM=desk

# ─── The team's computer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One persistent machine shared by every Bot: a browser per Bot that you can
# watch and take over, a terminal, and files.
# Where it runs: vercel (default, in development too) | local (Docker on this
# machine: a standalone server, or development). Vercel needs credentials
# locally: `vercel link && vercel env pull`.
# BOT_COMPUTER=vercel
# Local runs in Docker by default, and the console watches it live: a small
# forwarder container publishes the computer's gateway on 127.0.0.1 only.
# `microsandbox` (or `vm`) runs a local VM instead, for development only: the
# console cannot open it.
# BOT_COMPUTER_LOCAL=docker
# Where the computer's software comes from. Locally, eve builds
# agent/sandbox/Dockerfile (Chrome, the desktop, agent-browser) by default. On
# Vercel, set a Vercel Container Registry image built from it, for linux/amd64:
#   vercel vcr build docker agent/sandbox --push
# Without one the computer installs its software the first time it starts.
# `default` uses eve's base image and that first-boot install anywhere.
# BOT_COMPUTER_IMAGE=
# An ordinary container forbids the user namespaces Chrome's sandbox needs, so
# Chrome runs without it there. This docker wrapper starts the computer with a
# seccomp profile (docker/seccomp-chrome.json) that allows them, and Chrome
# keeps its sandbox. Docker Compose sets it by default.
# EVE_DOCKER_PATH=./scripts/docker-chrome-sandbox.sh
# BOT_COMPUTER_LOCAL_PORT=16080
# Where the live view is published: this machine only by default. 0.0.0.0 (or
# one of this machine's addresses) lets people on other devices watch too; the
# console must then be served over plain http, or the gateway put behind TLS,
# since a browser will not open a ws:// connection from an https page.
# BOT_COMPUTER_LOCAL_BIND=127.0.0.1
# BOT_COMPUTER_FORWARDER_IMAGE=alpine/socat
# A new name starts a new machine.
# BOT_COMPUTER_NAME=bot-computer
# Vercel only: keep the team's work (shared, bots, downloads) and the browser
# profiles on a Vercel Drive named after the computer. A lost machine then loses
# nothing, sign-ins included: its replacement mounts the same drive. Backups
# become hourly. An existing machine mounts the drive from its next start.
# BOT_COMPUTER_DRIVE=on
# Drive size in GB; Vercel's default when unset.
# BOT_COMPUTER_DRIVE_GB=
# Screens (one per Bot at a time), reclaimed from the least recently used Bot.
# BOT_COMPUTER_MAX_SCREENS=4
# Stop a browser nobody has used for this long; it restarts on the next use.
# BOT_COMPUTER_IDLE_MINUTES=15
# Longest one person may hold a Bot's browser in one go.
# BOT_COMPUTER_CONTROL_MINUTES=30
# Live connections carry single-use tokens signed with this key. Generated and
# stored on first use when unset; set it (32+ characters) to pin it.
# BOT_COMPUTER_KEY=
# BOT_COMPUTER_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=60
# Extra Google Chrome flags, e.g. --lang=en-GB.
# BOT_COMPUTER_CHROME_FLAGS=
# Where packages come from when the sandbox cannot reach Ubuntu's own archive
# (Vercel Sandbox cannot). Must be an Ubuntu mirror.
# BOT_COMPUTER_APT_MIRROR=https://mirrors.edge.kernel.org/ubuntu
# Archive the computer to storage this often while Bots work (and at every job's end).
# 60 by default with BOT_COMPUTER_DRIVE.
# BOT_BACKUP_EVERY_MINUTES=10
# BOT_BACKUP_MAX_MB=512
# 2 GB of memory per vCPU; each running browser takes roughly 0.5 GB.
# BOT_SANDBOX_VCPUS=4
# BOT_SANDBOX_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=30
# Comma-separated egress allow-list. Unset = allow all (needed for first-run
# browser install). Bump BOT_SANDBOX_REVISION to force a template rebuild.
# BOT_SANDBOX_ALLOW_DOMAINS=
# BOT_SANDBOX_REVISION=0

# ─── The bot's browser ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BOT_BROWSER_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=
# Route every Bot's browser through a proxy (applied as a Chrome flag).
# BOT_BROWSER_PROXY=
# BOT_BROWSER_MAX_OUTPUT=20000
# Set to 0 to skip baking the browser and display into the sandbox template.
# BOT_SANDBOX_PREINSTALL_BROWSER=1

# ─── Outbound email (human-approved) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset = send_email returns a draft instead of sending.
# The relay must dedupe on the Idempotency-Key header and answer 2xx with a
# receipt id; see README, "Outbound email relay".
# BOT_EMAIL_WEBHOOK=
# BOT_EMAIL_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=
# How long one relay request may take before its outcome counts as unknown.
# BOT_EMAIL_WEBHOOK_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# ─── Spend caps ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A job started from a thread gets at most what the thread has left.
# BOT_HQ_COST_LIMIT_USD=10
# BOT_JOB_COST_LIMIT_USD=5
# Workspace budget per UTC month (and optionally per UTC day), across HQ, every
# job run, routine cycles, retries, Jev and memory. Each run reserves an
# estimate for its effort (quick $0.25, standard $1.50, deep $4, never above
# BOT_JOB_COST_LIMIT_USD) before it starts and gives back what it did not
# spend; a run that would not fit is blocked with the reason. Unset or 0 = no
# limit; spend is still tracked and shown on the usage page.
# BOT_BUDGET_MONTHLY_USD=
# BOT_BUDGET_DAILY_USD=
#
# The computer's runtime (Vercel Sandbox) counts against the same budget once
# you give it a rate: the watchdog charges each finished session once, to the
# workspace. Take the figures from your Vercel plan's Sandbox pricing; nothing
# here assumes one. Any combination, in USD: per wall-clock hour the computer
# runs, per hour of active CPU as Vercel reports it, and per GB of provisioned
# memory per hour. Unset = counted as unpriced.
# BOT_COMPUTER_USD_PER_HOUR=
# BOT_COMPUTER_USD_PER_CPU_HOUR=
# BOT_COMPUTER_USD_PER_GB_HOUR=

# ─── Bots on Claude Code or Codex (experimental) ────────────────────────────
# Off by default. Names the engines a Bot may run on besides eve (claude-code,
# codex, or both); each job then gets a Vercel Sandbox of its own with no
# network. Billed through AI Gateway like any Bot, never a Claude or ChatGPT
# subscription. Read at build; not on Hobby. Settings → Engines in the console
# sets the same, and wins over these. See docs/configuration.md.
# BOT_ENGINES=claude-code,codex
# BOT_CLAUDE_CODE_MODEL=
# BOT_CODEX_MODEL=openai/gpt-5.4-mini
# A shell and file editing in the job's Sandbox (off unless 1), and what its
# network may reach (none unless set: allow-all, or a comma list of domains).
# BOT_ENGINE_BUILTINS=1
# BOT_ENGINE_NETWORK=registry.npmjs.org,*.github.com

# ─── QA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# On a scratch deployment only: opens the fault routes `npm run qa` uses to
# crash a job's run or replace the computer, so it can watch the recovery.
# BOT_QA=1
